om=sidebar&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39926&amp;iid=116724&amp;issue-id=116724&amp;jid=2&amp;journal-id=2&amp;FROM=Article%7CNavigation&amp;TO=Entrez%7CPubMed%7CAuthor%20Search&amp;rendering-type=normal&amp;&amp;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;db=PubMed&amp;term=%20Visscher+H%5Bauth%5D" class="sidefm-pmclink"> Visscher, H.</a></div><div class="sidefm-pmclink-item"><a href="redirect3.cgi?&amp;&amp;auth=0-WAzrvjPi6uOz-mjEnn4hgh1mhZ2CyD9FOvbikAY&amp;reftype=authsrch&amp;refto=entrez&amp;reffrom=sidebar&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39926&amp;iid=116724&amp;issue-id=116724&amp;jid=2&amp;journal-id=2&amp;FROM=Article%7CNavigation&amp;TO=Entrez%7CPubMed%7CAuthor%20Search&amp;rendering-type=normal&amp;&amp;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;db=PubMed&amp;term=%20Brinkhuis+H%5Bauth%5D" class="sidefm-pmclink"> Brinkhuis, H.</a></div><div class="sidefm-pmclink-item"><a href="redirect3.cgi?&amp;&amp;auth=0-QUwZ6V9D_I6-Ge7ouyAZAUcjlSnROIRPeVDZARc&amp;reftype=authsrch&amp;refto=entrez&amp;reffrom=sidebar&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39926&amp;iid=116724&amp;issue-id=116724&amp;jid=2&amp;journal-id=2&amp;FROM=Article%7CNavigation&amp;TO=Entrez%7CPubMed%7CAuthor%20Search&amp;rendering-type=normal&amp;&amp;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;db=PubMed&amp;term=%20Dilcher+DL%5Bauth%5D" class="sidefm-pmclink"> Dilcher, D.</a></div><div class="sidefm-pmclink-item"><a href="redirect3.cgi?&amp;&amp;auth=0z36lD7sKbk76luDiHdxsvAsAiR0BqAYHlTC2z567&amp;reftype=authsrch&amp;refto=entrez&amp;reffrom=sidebar&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39926&amp;iid=116724&amp;issue-id=116724&amp;jid=2&amp;journal-id=2&amp;FROM=Article%7CNavigation&amp;TO=Entrez%7CPubMed%7CAuthor%20Search&amp;rendering-type=normal&amp;&amp;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;db=PubMed&amp;term=%20Traverse+A%5Bauth%5D" class="sidefm-pmclink"> Traverse, A.</a></div></div></div></td><td rowspan="100" width="20px" height="300" background="corehtml/pmc/pmcgifs/wm-pnas.gif"><div style="width:20px"></div></td><td valign="top" class="content-cell"><div class="front-matter-section"><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" width="100%"><tr style="vertical-align: top"><td><div class="fm-citation"><div><span class="citation-abbreviation">Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. </span><span class="citation-publication-date">1996 March 5; </span><span class="citation-volume">93</span><span class="citation-issue">(5)</span><span class="citation-flpages">: 2155–2158. </span></div><div><span class="fm-vol-iss-date"> </span></div></div></td><td class="fm-citation-ids"><div class="fm-citation-pmcid"><span class="fm-citation-ids-label">PMCID: </span>PMC39926</div></td></tr></table><div class="fm-copyright"><a class="int-reflink" href="about/copyright.html">Copyright notice</a> </div><div class="fm-title">The terminal Paleozoic fungal event: evidence of terrestrial ecosystem destabilization and collapse.</div><div class="fm-author contrib-group">H Visscher, H Brinkhuis, D L Dilcher, W C Elsik, Y Eshet, C V Looy, M R Rampino, and  A Traverse<div class="fm-affl">Laboratory of Paleobotany and Palynology, Utrecht University, Utrecht, The Netherlands.</div></div><div class="fm-footnote"></div><div class="links-box"><div class="fm-footnote"><img src="corehtml/pmc/pmcgifs/rt-arrow.gif" alt="Small right arrow pointing to:" style="vertical-align: middle;"> This article has been <a class="int-reflink" href="tocrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;action=cited">cited by</a> other articles in PMC.</div></div><div class="summary"><div class="section-content"><div class="head1A section-title" id="id539986">Abstract</div>Because of its prominent role in global biomass storage, land vegetation is the most obvious biota to be investigated for records of dramatic ecologic crisis in Earth history. There is accumulating evidence that, throughout the world, sedimentary organic matter preserved in latest Permian deposits is characterized by unparalleled abundances of fungal remains, irrespective of depositional environment (marine, lacustrine, fluviatile), floral provinciality, and climatic zonation. This fungal event can be considered to reflect excessive dieback of arboreous vegetation, effecting destabilization and subsequent collapse of terrestrial ecosystems with concomitant loss of standing biomass. Such a scenario is in harmony with predictions that the Permian-Triassic ecologic crisis was triggered by the effects of severe changes in atmospheric chemistry arising from the rapid eruption of the Siberian Traps flood basalts.</div><div class="head1A section-title" id="id629728">Full text</div><div class="section-content">Full text is available as a scanned copy of the original print version. Get a printable copy (PDF file) of the <a class="int-reflink" href="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobtype=pdf"><strong>complete article</strong></a> (1.2M), or click on a page image below to browse page by page. Links to PubMed are also available for <a class="int-reflink" href="articlerender.fcgi?artid=39926#reference-sec"><strong>Selected References</strong></a>.</div><div class="section-content" style="margin-left: 5px; margin-top: 5px;"><div style="clear: both;"></div><div class="section-with-images-on-the-same-page-graphic"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=1"><img src="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobname=pnas01509-0430.gif" alt="icon of scanned page 2155" title="scanned icon of page 2155" border="1" width="100" class="icon-reflink"></a><div class="figure-role"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=1">2155</a></div></div><div class="section-with-images-on-the-same-page-graphic"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=2"><img src="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobname=pnas01509-0431.gif" alt="icon of scanned page 2156" title="scanned icon of page 2156" border="1" width="100" class="icon-reflink"></a><div class="figure-role"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=2">2156</a></div></div><div class="section-with-images-on-the-same-page-graphic"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=3"><img src="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobname=pnas01509-0432.gif" alt="icon of scanned page 2157" title="scanned icon of page 2157" border="1" width="100" class="icon-reflink"></a><div class="figure-role"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=3">2157</a></div></div><div class="section-with-images-on-the-same-page-graphic"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=4"><img src="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobname=pnas01509-0433.gif" alt="icon of scanned page 2158" title="scanned icon of page 2158" border="1" width="100" class="icon-reflink"></a><div class="figure-role"><a class="link" href="pagerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;pageindex=4">2158</a></div></div><div style="clear: both;"> </div><div style="clear: both;"></div></div><div class="section-content section-with-images-on-the-same-page"><div class="head1A section-title" id="id629728">Images in this article</div><div style="margin-left: 5px;margin-top: 5px;"><div class="section-with-images-on-the-same-page-graphic"><a class="int-reflink" href="articlerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;rendertype=figure&amp;id=F1" onclick="startTarget(this, 'figure', 1024, 800)"><img src="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobname=pnas01509-0431-a.gif" border="1" class="icon-reflink" alt="Fig. 1" title="Fig. 1"><br>Fig. 1<br>on p.2156</a></div><div class="section-with-images-on-the-same-page-graphic"><a class="int-reflink" href="articlerender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;rendertype=figure&amp;id=F2" onclick="startTarget(this, 'figure', 1024, 800)"><img src="picrender.fcgi?artid=39926&amp;blobname=pnas01509-0431-b.gif" border="1" class="icon-reflink" alt="Fig. 2" title="Fig. 2"><br>Fig. 2<br>on p.2156</a></div><div class="p" style="clear: both;">Click on the image to see a larger version.</div></div></div><div class="section-content"><div class="head1A section-title" id="reference-sec">Selected References</div></div><div class="back-matter-section">These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article.	</div><div class="back-matter-section"><ul class="back-ref-list" style="list-style-type: disc;"><li id="OCR_00482">Stanley, SM; Yang, X. A Double Mass Extinction at the End of the Paleozoic Era. <span><span class="ref-journal">Science. </span>1994 Nov 25;<span class="ref-vol">266</span>(5189):1340–1344.</span>  <span style="white-space: nowrap;">[<a class="ref-extlink" href="redirect3.cgi?&amp;&amp;auth=0xu6WVPEHmV5MheQcn5XhkyDpi9i27tR9_j4Bu5O2&amp;reftype=pubmed&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39926&amp;iid=116724&amp;issue-id=116724&amp;jid=2&amp;journal-id=2&amp;FROM=Article%7CCitationRef&amp;TO=Entrez%7CPubMed%7CRecord&amp;rendering-type=normal&amp;&amp;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/17772839">PubMed</a>]</span></li><li id="OCR_00503">Labandeira, CC; Sepkoski, JJ., Jr Insect diversity in the fossil record. <span><span class="ref-journal">Science. </span>1993 Jul 16;<span class="ref-vol">261</span>(5119):310–315.</span>  <span style="white-space: nowrap;">[<a class="ref-extlink" href="redirect3.cgi?&amp;&amp;auth=0poPtNpVadvN3a0wtmPZtS7tQ3c_FJ_pYntgGeAmD&amp;reftype=pubmed&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39926&amp;iid=116724&amp;issue-id=116724&amp;jid=2&amp;journal-id=2&amp;FROM=Article%7CCitationRef&amp;TO=Entrez%7CPubMed%7CRecord&amp;rendering-type=normal&amp;&amp;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/11536548">PubMed</a>]</span></li><li id="OCR_00507">Retallack, GJ. Permian-Triassic Life Crisis on Land. <span><span class="ref-journal">Science. </span>1995 Jan 6;<span class="ref-vol">267</span>(5194):77–80.</span>  <span style="white-space: nowrap;">[<a class="ref-extlink" href="redirect3.cgi?&amp;&amp;auth=0VGwMHcGvKkQlvNWgZFpsQRWNZftuxUaFJhMrFAGj&amp;reftype=pubmed&amp;artid=39926&amp;article-id=39